robin wrote on Sun, 02 Nov 2003 21:55:24 GMT:
> It may be best to view the Clan as three trophies:

>        highest scoring clan
>        clan with the most unique deaths
>        "highest ranked" clan

> Unless someone has a good idea on how to add unique deaths and
> overall points scored into a single scoring formula?

Add them with a weight of zero :-)

Seriously: unique deaths should be an individual "achievement" in my
opinion: a fun prize, but not worth requiring clans to direct
resources at it; the clan with the best players should have the best
chances to win, not the one with the most persistent diers. (Fun ideas
for secondary clan prizes will surely emerge if the clan thing gets
going.)

Similarly, "highest scoring clan" makes no sense, since the score may
come out of multiple attempts of many badly scoring players--you
probably don't want to reward that.

So it should be a single ranking function that ranks the clans at the
end of the tournament, and it should account for the player's
achievements, not the average score. (If we took average score, not
only will clan Marvin most probably win, it will discourage clans
taking new members and such.) In other words you want a function that
rises monotonously throughout the tournament.

An idea would be to adapt the F1 system:

For each class, give the team 10pts if one of their members is 1st,
8pts if a team member is 2nd, 6pts if a member is 3rd, 5pts if a
member is 4th, and so on.

Additionally, small bonuses might be provided for the fastest
ascension, the lowest-scoring ascension, for each conduct that is
realized (but not for each ascension with the conduct). Perhaps with
some exclusions for conducts and/or a weight function that takes the
type of conduct and the number of other conducts ascended
simultaneously within a clan ascension into account). 

Such a system would require maximum versatility, would extend the
battle to all classes (not just "handy" ones, like wizards), and even
though it requires some number of members for a team to be
competitive, it does encourage solely good play (score-wise).

